{"parentUuid":"9dc24c60-9a4b-44b3-a537-9e89a38c2d2b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a058676b8e5461ac3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgM52oBY5DWTcr1s4ssFP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EfQT8Zq7JwguhzZf77PSBY","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"Round 8 review of `origin/master..HEAD` (orca.nix, tests/orca-cli-skills.sh, tests/sandbox-fonts.sh). Read-only: I wrote nothing, ran no tests, and killed nothing. To check claims I read the store artifacts already built for this branch: the forwardSignals GUI build `/nix/store/j0qfjf54…-orca-1.4.238`, the CLI bwrap launcher, the nixGL wrapper, AppRun and appimage-exec.sh. I also ran two one-line probes against nixpkgs' bash.\n\n## Critical\nNone.\n\n## Important\n\n**1. tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:226 — `compgen` does not exist in CI's bash, so `outside()` quietly stops removing ORCA_* variables.**\n- CI runs the test as `nix shell $tools#bash … -c bash \"$t\"`. That `bash` is nixpkgs' non-interactive bash, built without readline or programmable completion.\n- I checked the runtimeShell bash (`/nix/store/nnm9…-bash-5.3p20`): `type compgen` says \"not found\" and `compgen -e` says \"command not found\".\n- So `env $(compgen -e | …)` runs with no `-u` arguments, and the \"compgen: command not found\" message ends up inside `$got` (via the `2>&1` on lines 228 and 234).\n- In CI this happens to pass, because there are no ORCA_* variables and the `grep -w` / `grep '^# Orca CLI'` checks still match.\n- But the claim \"none of its env\" is not enforced by any bash without progcomp. Run from an Orca terminal under such a bash, the outside-Orca cases would keep ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_TOKEN and the other variables, and could pass without testing anything.\n- The value is never printed, so nothing leaks.\n- Fix: `${!ORCA_@}` works in that same bash (I tested it). For example: `outside() { local u=() v; for v in ${!ORCA_@}; do u+=(-u \"$v\"); done; env \"${u[@]}\" PATH=\"$clean_path\" \"$@\"; }`.\n\n**2. tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:142-148 together with orca.nix:21-23 and :52-53 — the 50ms early-TERM case falls inside a window the hold does not cover.**\n- `fwd` only holds while `/proc/$child/task/$child/children` has an entry. It breaks out and signals `$child` when that list is empty.\n- `$child` is still the buildFHSEnv launcher bash for a while. It forks `readlink` once per rootfs symlink plus `$(pwd)`, then execs bwrap, and bwrap only forks after that.\n- Every layer there has TERM ignored. A TERM that hits the empty list (between those forks, or before bwrap forks) goes to an ignoring process and is lost. The launcher then runs for the payload's full 20s, and the case fails after 10s.\n- So the test passes only if bwrap has forked within 50ms. That is probably true on a fast desktop, but on a loaded 4-core CI runner it is a flake source. This is the round-6 accepted race, but the round-7 test now depends on landing just after it.\n- The comment at :21-23 (\"until then fwd holds a signal rather than send it into an ignoring layer\") overstates the fix for the same reason.\n- I did not measure the timing. That is why I rate this Important rather than Critical.\n- Cheap fix that makes the test deterministic: inside the same 5s budget, keep looping while `kill -0 $child` succeeds and the children list is empty (instead of breaking), and only fall back to `$child` once the budget runs out.\n\n## Nits\n- **orca.nix:27** — \"A group-wide HUP or TERM arrives twice.\" A group-wide INT sent off a terminal (for example `kill -INT -pgid`) also arrives twice, because the foreground check does not suppress it.\n- **orca.nix:15-16** — A KillMode=control-group stop signals every process in the cgroup, not a process group. The effect is the same; only the wording (\"whole group\") is loose.\n- **tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:130-132** — Bad reflow: \"reaches\" is left alone on its own line.\n- **tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:28** — Only an EXIT trap. If the user Ctrl-Cs the suite, the payloads (in their own process group via `set -m`) keep running up to their 20s self-exit. It is bounded, and their writes go to already-removed files, so it does no harm.\n\n## Verified correct\n- **orca.nix:9-11** \"bwrap's one child is the payload itself, every layer below exec'ing the next\" holds.\n  - The current launcher has no `--unshare-pid` and no `--new-session`.\n  - container-init forks only ldconfig (a grandchild of bwrap) and then execs stage 2.\n  - The FHS init execs appimage-exec.sh, which execs AppRun. AppRun execs Electron from its EXIT trap, and resources/bin/orca-ide execs Electron too.\n- **orca.nix:42-44** The `/proc/$$/stat` field indices are right (st[2]=pgrp, st[5]=tpgid). The `##*) ` strip handles parentheses in comm.\n- **orca.nix:51** \"No trailing newline there: read fails yet still sets kid\" is accurate.\n- **Re-raise logic (:63-70)** The 127 \"already reaped\" handling and the 129/130/143-only re-raise match their comments.\n- **Merged orca.nix** reads coherently: one `let`, and appimageContents is shared exactly as the comment at :174-176 says. The odd line break at :81 comes from master.\n- **The test never prints secret values.** `outside` only builds variable names, and no case dumps `env` or uses `set -x`.\n- **Background cleanup works.** Every payload exits by itself (20s, or 4s for the alive case). The failing early case SIGKILLs the launcher, and `--die-with-parent` (pdeathsig on bwrap and its child) takes the payload down with it.\n- **Each signal case fails on a regression of its claim:**\n  - TERM, INT and group TERM all fail via rc 143.\n  - SigIgn fails if the env flags are dropped.\n  - The 148 case fails as `timeout` → 124.\n  - stdin fails if `<&0` is dropped.\n  - GUI lifetime fails if the plain exec comes back.\n- **No tty needed:** `set -m` makes its own process group without one in nixpkgs bash (I checked).\n- **CI tools:** apart from `compgen` above, everything the test uses is in CI's tool shell; python3 is skipped.\n- **tests/sandbox-fonts.sh:** the usage comment matches the rename, and its closure search for `.orca-ide-unwrapped` works with the nixGL-wrapped path.\n- **Security:** nothing beyond the above. The heredoc is `'EOF'`-quoted, every path is a store path, the only `/proc` reads are of the launcher's own child, and there is no private data."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":491,"cache_read_input_tokens":89275,"output_tokens":3085,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":650},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":491},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3085,"cache_read_input_tokens":89275,"cache_creation_input_tokens":491,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":491,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01EfQT8Zq7JwguhzZf77PSBY":{"message":"Round 8 review of `origin/master..HEAD` (orca.nix, tests/orca-cli-skills.sh, tests/sandbox-fonts.sh). Read-only: I wrote nothing, ran no tests, and killed nothing. To check claims I read the store artifacts already built for this branch: the forwardSignals GUI build `/nix/store/j0qfjf54…-orca-1.4.238`, the CLI bwrap launcher, the nixGL wrapper, AppRun and appimage-exec.sh. I also ran two one-line probes against nixpkgs' bash.\n\n## Critical\nNone.\n\n## Important\n\n**1. tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:226 — `compgen` does not exist in CI's bash, so `outside()` quietly stops removing ORCA_* variables.**\n- CI runs the test as `nix shell $tools#bash … -c bash \"$t\"`. That `bash` is nixpkgs' non-interactive bash, built without readline or programmable completion.\n- I checked the runtimeShell bash (`/nix/store/nnm9…-bash-5.3p20`): `type compgen` says \"not found\" and `compgen -e` says \"command not found\".\n- So `env $(compgen -e | …)` runs with no `-u` arguments, and the \"compgen: command not found\" message ends up inside `$got` (via the `2>&1` on lines 228 and 234).\n- In CI this happens to pass, because there are no ORCA_* variables and the `grep -w` / `grep '^# Orca CLI'` checks still match.\n- But the claim \"none of its env\" is not enforced by any bash without progcomp. Run from an Orca terminal under such a bash, the outside-Orca cases would keep ORCA_AGENT_HOOK_TOKEN and the other variables, and could pass without testing anything.\n- The value is never printed, so nothing leaks.\n- Fix: `${!ORCA_@}` works in that same bash (I tested it). For example: `outside() { local u=() v; for v in ${!ORCA_@}; do u+=(-u \"$v\"); done; env \"${u[@]}\" PATH=\"$clean_path\" \"$@\"; }`.\n\n**2. tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:142-148 together with orca.nix:21-23 and :52-53 — the 50ms early-TERM case falls inside a window the hold does not cover.**\n- `fwd` only holds while `/proc/$child/task/$child/children` has an entry. It breaks out and signals `$child` when that list is empty.\n- `$child` is still the buildFHSEnv launcher bash for a while. It forks `readlink` once per rootfs symlink plus `$(pwd)`, then execs bwrap, and bwrap only forks after that.\n- Every layer there has TERM ignored. A TERM that hits the empty list (between those forks, or before bwrap forks) goes to an ignoring process and is lost. The launcher then runs for the payload's full 20s, and the case fails after 10s.\n- So the test passes only if bwrap has forked within 50ms. That is probably true on a fast desktop, but on a loaded 4-core CI runner it is a flake source. This is the round-6 accepted race, but the round-7 test now depends on landing just after it.\n- The comment at :21-23 (\"until then fwd holds a signal rather than send it into an ignoring layer\") overstates the fix for the same reason.\n- I did not measure the timing. That is why I rate this Important rather than Critical.\n- Cheap fix that makes the test deterministic: inside the same 5s budget, keep looping while `kill -0 $child` succeeds and the children list is empty (instead of breaking), and only fall back to `$child` once the budget runs out.\n\n## Nits\n- **orca.nix:27** — \"A group-wide HUP or TERM arrives twice.\" A group-wide INT sent off a terminal (for example `kill -INT -pgid`) also arrives twice, because the foreground check does not suppress it.\n- **orca.nix:15-16** — A KillMode=control-group stop signals every process in the cgroup, not a process group. The effect is the same; only the wording (\"whole group\") is loose.\n- **tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:130-132** — Bad reflow: \"reaches\" is left alone on its own line.\n- **tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:28** — Only an EXIT trap. If the user Ctrl-Cs the suite, the payloads (in their own process group via `set -m`) keep running up to their 20s self-exit. It is bounded, and their writes go to already-removed files, so it does no harm.\n\n## Verified correct\n- **orca.nix:9-11** \"bwrap's one child is the payload itself, every layer below exec'ing the next\" holds.\n  - The current launcher has no `--unshare-pid` and no `--new-session`.\n  - container-init forks only ldconfig (a grandchild of bwrap) and then execs stage 2.\n  - The FHS init execs appimage-exec.sh, which execs AppRun. AppRun execs Electron from its EXIT trap, and resources/bin/orca-ide execs Electron too.\n- **orca.nix:42-44** The `/proc/$$/stat` field indices are right (st[2]=pgrp, st[5]=tpgid). The `##*) ` strip handles parentheses in comm.\n- **orca.nix:51** \"No trailing newline there: read fails yet still sets kid\" is accurate.\n- **Re-raise logic (:63-70)** The 127 \"already reaped\" handling and the 129/130/143-only re-raise match their comments.\n- **Merged orca.nix** reads coherently: one `let`, and appimageContents is shared exactly as the comment at :174-176 says. The odd line break at :81 comes from master.\n- **The test never prints secret values.** `outside` only builds variable names, and no case dumps `env` or uses `set -x`.\n- **Background cleanup works.** Every payload exits by itself (20s, or 4s for the alive case). The failing early case SIGKILLs the launcher, and `--die-with-parent` (pdeathsig on bwrap and its child) takes the payload down with it.\n- **Each signal case fails on a regression of its claim:**\n  - TERM, INT and group TERM all fail via rc 143.\n  - SigIgn fails if the env flags are dropped.\n  - The 148 case fails as `timeout` → 124.\n  - stdin fails if `<&0` is dropped.\n  - GUI lifetime fails if the plain exec comes back.\n- **No tty needed:** `set -m` makes its own process group without one in nixpkgs bash (I checked).\n- **CI tools:** apart from `compgen` above, everything the test uses is in CI's tool shell; python3 is skipped.\n- **tests/sandbox-fonts.sh:** the usage comment matches the rename, and its closure search for `.orca-ide-unwrapped` works with the nixGL-wrapped path.\n- **Security:** nothing beyond the above. The heredoc is `'EOF'`-quoted, every path is a store path, the only `/proc` reads are of the launcher's own child, and there is no private data."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"c98fbdec-fe33-4e79-86c2-a37f570eaf95","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgM52nsgbc3AAYEhKSjDA","requestRef":"d92f65b2-7af4-4ac7-a0fc-f54845bd0f28","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionAgent":"Explore","type":"assistant","uuid":"0fb20f41-26fa-4e29-8278-177bfdde96a5","timestamp":"2026-10-24T12:35:08.704Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/sauyon/devel/dotfiles/.drovr/wt/orca-cli-skills","sessionId":"d19ff9ff-38c8-4378-ac54-4f4061f74300","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"orca-cli-skills"}
